"Equality of group and group-list edge chromatic numbers | MathDB (https://mathdb.com/p/321197/equality-of-group-and-group-list-edge-chromatic-numbers)\nciteturn0search0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; # Equality of group and group-list edge chromatic numbers ... This is presented alongside the proposed analogue of Vizing's theorem as a conjecture about the coincidence of group edge choice parameters.\n\n# Equality of group and group-list edge chromatic numbers\n\nStatus\n    open\n\nImportance\n    Research corpus\n\nSolutions\n    0\n\nAbout 15 years old · traced to 2011\n\nLet $G$G be a simple graph. Denote its group edge chromatic number by $\\chi'_g(G)$χ g′​(G) and its group list edge chromatic number by $\\chi'_{gl}(G)$χ g l′​(G). Equality conjecture. For every simple graph $G$G,\n\n$$\\chi'_g(G)=\\chi'_{gl}(G).$$χ g′​(G)=χ g l′​(G).\n\nThis is presented alongside the proposed analogue of Vizing's theorem as a conjecture about the coincidence of group edge choice parameters. The supplied status evidence marks the candidate as resolved, but gives no identifying theorem or reference.\n\nReferences\n\n## Primary source\n\nXin Zhang and Guizhen Liu, “Group edge choosability of planar graphs without adjacent short cycles”, arXiv:1102.4980 (2011).\n\n## Additional references\n\n2 papers in this index state this conjecture (2011). The statement above is taken from the most recent of them; the others are arXiv:1101.0053.\n\nedge-coloringgraph-coloring\n\n0 0\n\nPosted 1 month ago\n\n## Progress summary\n\nRefresh Never refreshed\n\nNothing recorded yet. Refresh searches the literature and the public web for attempts on this problem, and writes the first summary here.\n\n## Solutions 0\n\n[Button: Post a solution]\n\n[Button: Solve with an agent]\n\nNo solutions have been posted yet.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nag.algebraic geometry - Reconstructing a function from its variants that negate one argument - MathOverflow (https://mathoverflow.net/questions/163917/reconstructing-a-function-from-its-variants-that-negate-one-argument)\nciteturn0search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: 12.6 years ago; Crawled: 4 months ago; To show that the question is hard in general, even for polynomials, I'll note that it includes Stanley's unsolved switching reconstruction conjecture for graphs. ... In the case of $n=4$, there is a counterexample: consider a 4-cycle and the graph with no edges.\n\nTo show that the question is hard in general, even for polynomials, I'll note that it includes Stanley's unsolved switching reconstruction conjecture for graphs. For a graph $G$ with $n$ vertices and edge set $E$, define $$f(x_1,\\ldots,x_n) = \\prod_{i<j;~ ij\\in E} (1+x_ix_y) \\prod_{i<j;~ij\\notin E}(1-x_ix_j).$$ Now $f_i$ corresponds to switching $G$ at vertex $i$. It is unknown whether $G$ can be reconstructed from $\\mathcal D(f)$ for $n\\gt 4$. In the case of $n=4$, there is a counterexample: consider a 4-cycle and the graph with no edges.\n\nThe choice of complex numbers was arbitrary; you can consider any domain and range for which the question is meaningful.\n\nSome facts can be determined by the methods used for other reconstruction problems. For example, if $n$ is odd then the equivalence class of $f$ can be reconstructed from $\\mathcal D(f)$: it is the only equivalence class occurring an odd number of times in the multiset union of the multisets $\\mathcal D(f_i)$. Beáta Faller and I will publish some results on symmetries of non-reconstructible functions. But what is known already?\n\n  * ag.algebraic-geometry\n  * reference-request\n  * graph-theory\n  * graph-reconstruction\n\nasked Apr 21, 2014 at 5:24\n\nBrendan McKay\n\n38.8k 3 3 gold badges 86 86 silver badges 155 155 bronze badges\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSwitching reconstruction of digraphs | UnsolvedMath (https://www.unsolvedmath.com/problems/OPG-46952)\nciteturn0search2 [wordlim: 200] Published: 13.7 years ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; The collection of switchings of a digraph $D$D is called the switching deck of $D$D. ... The problem is a directed analogue of switching reconstruction of graphs in which one complements the edges at a vertex, instead of reversing each of its incident arcs. ... Switching reconstruction conjecture ... Find a verified later classification/counterexample for orders divisible by four.\n\nOPG-46952\n\nPartially Solved\n\nL1\n\n# Switching reconstruction of digraphs\n\nCategory:Graph Theory\n\nSet:OpenGarden\n\n## Problem Statement\n\nQuestion Are there any switching-nonreconstructible digraphs on twelve or more vertices?\n\nCopy LaTeX source\n\n## Background\n\nSource: Open Problem Garden. Original node ID: 46952. URL: http://www.openproblemgarden.org/op/switching_reconstruction_of_digraphs.\n\nSource subject path: Graph Theory.\n\nSource importance: Medium ✭✭.\n\nDiscussion and literature:\nSource-derived literature/context:\n- Source: http://www.openproblemgarden.org/op/switching_reconstruction_of_digraphs\n- Author(s): Bondy, J. Adrian; Mercier, Fabien\n- Subject(s): Graph Theory\n- Importance: Medium ✭✭\n- Recommended for undergraduates: no\n- Posted: March 7th, 2013 by fhavet\n\nProblem-page discussion:\nTo switch a vertex of a digraph is to reverse all the arcs incident to it. The digraph so obtained is called a switching of the digraph. The collection of switchings of a digraph $D$D is called the switching deck of $D$D. \nDiscussion links:\n- switching reconstruction of graphs: http://www.openproblemgarden.org/?q=node/46951\n- Stanley's conjecture: http://www.openproblemgarden.org/?q=node/46951\n\nComputation notes:\nFinite compute signal:\n- No local finite computation has been run for this source-derived note.\n- This record was generated from the Open Problem Garden page text, discussion, bibliography, and comments.\n- LaTeX expressions extracted from the source text: 3.\n\n## Literature Review\n\nChecked 2026-08-17\n\nPartially solved\n\nCurrent literature assessment. Bondy--Mercier prove reconstruction when n is not congruent to zero modulo four, but the searched sources did not determine whether a switching-nonreconstructible digraph exists at an order at least twelve.\n\nVerified partial progress.\n\n  * Bondy--Mercier establish switching reconstruction for digraph orders n not congruent to 0 modulo 4.\n\nFull solution or refutation.\n\nThat congruence theorem leaves the exact existential question at n >= 12 unresolved in this review.\n\nWhat remains.\n\nFind a verified later classification/counterexample for orders divisible by four.\n\nSources checked.\n\n- J. A. Bondy and F. Mercier, Switching reconstruction of digraphs, Journal of Graph Theory 67 (2011), 332--348. (primary): https://doi.org/10.1002/jgt.20512\nEvidence used: The problem record summarizes their theorem for n not congruent to 0 modulo 4.\n- Open Problem Garden, Switching reconstruction of digraphs (accessed 2026-08-17). (maintained_tracker): https://garden.irmacs.sfu.ca/op/switching_reconstruction_of_digraphs\nEvidence used: States the exact question and the congruence-class theorem.\n\nReview notes. No source alteration; the unanswered congruence class is not treated as a resolution.\n\nThis is a dated literature triage; an open classification is not proof that no later or unindexed result exists.\n\n\n## Discussion (1)\n\n### th0rgal\n\n08:02 on 4 Aug 2026\n\nAlready resolved, the answer is YES.\n\nMcKay and Schweitzer give two non-isomorphic digraphs on 12 vertices with the same switching deck; see Figure 12 of their paper.\n\nAn independent reconstruction of the pair verifies that:\n\n  * the two digraphs are not isomorphic;\n  * each switching deck contains 12 cards;\n  * in both cases, the cards fall into the same three isomorphism classes;\n  * each class occurs with multiplicity 4.\n\nThus the two digraphs have identical switching decks and are switching-nonreconstructible.\n\nReference:\n\nB. D. McKay and P. Schweitzer, \"Switching Reconstruction of Digraphs,\" Journal of Graph Theory 76 (2014), no. 4, 279–296.\n\nDOI: https://doi.org/10.1002/jgt.21765\n\nTherefore the question, as literally stated for general digraphs, has an affirmative answer.\n\nImportant qualification: the examples contain digons (pairs of oppositely directed arcs). The corresponding question restricted to oriented graphs with no directed 2-cycles remains open.\n\nSign in to join the discussion and contribute your insights.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPII: 0095-8956(85)90078-4 (https://math.mit.edu/~rstan/pubs/pubfiles/63.pdf)\nciteturn0search12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 23.0 years ago; Crawled: 19.2 years ago; The well-known Kelly–Ulam vertex-reconstruction conjecture (see [1] for a survey up to 1977) asks whether \\(X\\) can be uniquely reconstructed from its unlabeled vertex-deleted subgraphs when \\(n\\ne 2\\). ... **Vertex-Switching Reconstruction Problem.\nJOURNAL OF COMBINATORIAL THEORY, Series B 38, 132–138 (1985)\n\n# Reconstruction from Vertex-Switching\n\n**Richard P. Stanley***\n\nDepartment of Mathematics, Massachusetts Institute of Technology,  \nCambridge, Massachusetts 02139\n\nCommunicated by C. Godsil\n\nReceived May 15, 1984\n\nLet \\(X\\) be a graph with vertices \\(x_1,\\ldots,x_n\\). Let \\(X_i\\) be the graph obtained by removing all edges \\(\\{x_i,x_j\\}\\) of \\(X\\) and inserting all nonedges \\(\\{x_i,x_k\\}\\). If \\(n\\not\\equiv 0\\pmod 4\\), then \\(X\\) can be uniquely reconstructed from the unlabeled graphs \\(X_1,\\ldots,X_n\\). If \\(n=4\\) the result is false, while for \\(n=4m\\geq 8\\) the result remains open. The proof uses linear algebra and does not explicitly describe the reconstructed graph \\(X\\). © 1985 Academic Press, Inc.\n\n## 1. Introduction\n\nLet \\(X\\) be a graph (with no loops or multiple edges) on the vertex set \\(\\{x_1,\\ldots,x_n\\}\\). The well-known Kelly–Ulam vertex-reconstruction conjecture (see [1] for a survey up to 1977) asks whether \\(X\\) can be uniquely reconstructed from its unlabeled vertex-deleted subgraphs when \\(n\\ne 2\\). Here we consider a variation of this problem where vertex-deletion is replaced by *vertex-switching*. More precisely, let \\(X_i\\) be the graph obtained from \\(X\\) by *switching* at vertex \\(x_i\\) [6, 7], i.e., by deleting all edges of \\(X\\) incident to \\(x_i\\) and inserting all possible edges incident to \\(x_i\\) which are not in \\(X\\).\n\n**Vertex-Switching Reconstruction Problem.** Can \\(X\\) be uniquely reconstructed from the unlabeled graphs \\(X_1,\\ldots,X_n\\)? In other words, if \\(X'\\) is another graph on the same vertex set and if \\(X_i\\cong X'_i\\) for \\(1\\leq i\\leq n\\), then is \\(X\\cong X'\\)?\n\n**1.1. Example.** Let \\(X=4K_1\\), the totally disconnected graph on four vertices, and let \\(X'=C_4\\), a cycle of length 4. Then for any \\(i\\), \\(X_i\\) and \\(X'_i\\) are isomorphic to the star (or claw) \\(K_{1,3}\\). Hence the vertex-switching reconstruction problem has a negative answer in general.\n\n* Partially supported by NSF Grant MCS-8104855 and by a Guggenheim fellowship.\n\n132\n\n0095-8956/85 $3.00  \nCopyright © 1985 by Academic Press, Inc.  \nAll rights of reproduction in any form reserved.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n(b) Suppose \\(N = 4t+2\\). If \\(G_Z \\sim G\\), Lemma 5.7 shows that \\(D_{2t}(G)=D_{2t+2}(G_Z)=D_{2t+2}(G)\\). Therefore, by Lemma 5.14(c) with \\(k=2t+1\\), \\(D_1(G)\\) determines \\((N+2)D_{2t}(G)\\) and thus \\(D_{2t}(G)\\). Since by Lemma 5.15(b) we can determine \\(\\mathcal{X}_t(G)=D_{2t}(G)-(-1)^t\\binom{2t+1}{t}[G]\\) from \\(D_1(G)\\), we can solve for \\([G]\\). (https://ora.ox.ac.uk/objects/uuid%3A5b6d514c-241f-4a77-8fa2-55fd069df278/files/dbg257f65g)\nciteturn0search13 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3.2 years ago; With Stanley’s switchings, we do have \\(G_Z \\sim G\\), so statements (a) and (b) of the preceding theorem essentially give an alternative derivation of the Alon-Coppersmith argument. ... Unfortunately though, this also means that the open cases of the Switching Reconstruction Conjecture are not handled by case (c).\nUnfortunately though, this also means that the open cases of the Switching Reconstruction Conjecture are not handled by case (c).\n\n**Theorem 5.17.** *Suppose that \\(N=2m\\) and \\(G \\in \\mathcal{G}\\) is not reconstructible. Then, for each even \\(j\\), there are at least \\(\\binom{m}{j}\\) sets \\(W \\subseteq Z\\) such that \\(|W|=2j\\) and \\(G_W \\sim G\\). Moreover, for any \\(z \\in Z\\), at least \\(\\binom{m-1}{j-1}\\) such sets include \\(z\\) and at least \\(\\binom{m-1}{j}\\) don’t. In total, there are at least \\(2^{m-1}\\) sets \\(W \\subseteq Z\\) such that \\(G_W \\sim G\\), including at least \\(2^{m-2}\\) sets which include \\(z\\) and \\(2^{m-2}\\) which don’t.*\n\n*Proof.* These claims follow from Lemma 5.15. For example, if there are fewer than \\(\\binom{m}{j}\\) sets \\(W\\) with \\(|W|=2j\\) and \\(G_W \\sim G\\), then \\([G]\\) is the unique element of \\(\\mathcal{X}_j\\) with negative multiplicity. For the totals, recall the identity \\(\\sum_{k\\ge 0}\\binom{K}{2k}=2^{K-1}\\).\n\nGiven a switching operation \\((\\phi,\\sim)\\), let us define the *redundancy* of \\(G \\in \\mathcal{G}\\) to be\n\\[\n\\rho(G)=\\max_{G' \\sim G}\\left\\{\\left| \\{W \\subseteq Z : |W| \\text{ is a multiple of } 4 \\text{ and } G_W = G'\\} \\right|\\right\\},\n\\]\nthat is, the maximum number of multiple-of-4 switchings producing the same (not just equivalent) result that is equivalent to \\(G\\). This brings us to the following sufficient condition for switching reconstructibility.\n\n**Theorem 5.18.** \\(G \\in \\mathcal{G}\\) is reconstructible if\n\\[\n\\left|\\{W : W \\subseteq Z,\\, G_W \\sim G,\\, \\text{and } |W| \\text{ is divisible by } 4\\}\\right| < 2^{N/2-1}.\n\\]\n\nIn particular, this is true if \\(\\rho(G)\\,|[G]| < 2^{N/2-1}\\).\n\n*Proof.* This follows from Theorem 5.16 if \\(N\\) is odd and Theorem 5.17 if \\(N\\) is even.\n\n84--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nNumerical Measures for Two-Graphs (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/2211845_Numerical_Measures_for_Two-Graphs)\nciteturn0search3 [wordlim: 200] Published: 18.0 years ago; Crawled: 3 months ago; introduces another new conjecture for switching equivalent classes in terms of decks ... reconstruction conjecture as stated in [10] is:\n\nIf every reconstruction of G is iso morphic to G, we say G is reco nstructible. The\n\nreconstruction conjecture as stated in [10] is:\n\nConjecture 1. Every graph with at least three vertices is reconstructible.\n\nWe call the switching equivalent class [[H]] reconstructible if D(G) ∼ D(H)\n\nimplies that G ∈ [[H]]. This leads to our switching equivalent recons truction con-\n\njecture.\n\nConjecture 2. Every switching equivalent class on at least 4 vertices is recon-\n\nstructible.\n\nA positive result for Conjecture 1 would prove Conjecture 2. However, these con-\n\njectures are not equivalent. Also, many of the classes of graphs for which Conjecture\n\nNUMERICAL MEASURES FOR TWO-GRAPHS 9\n\n1 is known, i.e. disconnected graphs, regular graphs, etc., can not be considered\n\nunder Conjecture 2 since their deﬁning properties are not preserved by switching.\n\nRevisiting the counterexamples from the previous sections, we see that decks\n\ndiﬀerentiate the switching equivalent clas ses.\n\nExample 5.3. Consider the graphs from Example 3.8. The graphs X\n\n1\n\nand X\n\n2\n\non\n\nsix vertices are not switching equivalent, and yet e\n\n∞\n\nm\n\n(X\n\n1\n\n) = e\n\n∞\n\nm\n\n(X\n\n2\n\n) for 1 ≤ m ≤ 6 .\n\n◦\n\n◦◦\n\n◦\n\n◦ ◦\n\n1\n\n1\n\n1\n\nM\n\nM\n\nM\n\nM\n\nM\n\nM\n\nq\n\nq\n\nq\n\nq\n\nq\n\nq\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n◦\n\n◦◦\n\n◦\n\n◦ ◦\n\n1\n\n1\n\n1\n\nM\n\nM\n\nM\n\nM\n